# SQL is always checked out with LF, whatever core.autocrlf says.
#
# D1's server-side statement splitting does not recognise a trigger body when
# BEGIN is followed by \r\n: it cuts the trigger at its first inner semicolon
# and answers "incomplete input". With core.autocrlf=true on Windows, any
# migration git writes out becomes CRLF, so the first migration to be both
# freshly checked out and to contain a trigger (0018) failed in production
# while passing locally, where real SQLite treats \r as whitespace. Found on
# 2026-09-15 by reproducing on a throwaway D1: the same file passes as LF and
# fails as CRLF, every time.
*.sql text eol=lf
